<h2>The jurisdictional point</h2>
<p>Section 482 BNSS speaks of a person having &ldquo;reason to believe that he may be arrested on accusation of having committed a non-bailable offence.&rdquo; Nothing in that language expires on the filing of a final report. <em>Bharat Chaudhary</em> made the point expressly: courts can grant anticipatory bail even when cognizance is taken or a chargesheet is filed, provided the facts require it. The earlier line of authority limiting the duration of anticipatory orders was read as guidance on how the discretion is exercised, not as a source of a jurisdictional bar.</p>

<h2>How the arguments reshuffle after the chargesheet</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <strong>Before the chargesheet</strong>
    <p>The State&rsquo;s strongest card is custodial interrogation — recoveries to be made, confrontations pending, a live money or evidence trail. The applicant leans on cooperation and the presumption of innocence.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <strong>After the chargesheet</strong>
    <p>Custodial interrogation is spent — the investigator has closed the file without needing the applicant in custody. The State leans instead on gravity, the specificity of the final report, and securing presence at trial. The applicant&rsquo;s non-arrest during the entire probe becomes the central equity.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Situations where the late application typically arises</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Practical note</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Never arrested during probe; summons or warrant now issued</td><td>The strongest fact pattern — investigation concluded without custody, so arrest now serves mainly ritual; courts frequently protect, directing appearance and bonds</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Named in a supplementary chargesheet</td><td>Treated close to a fresh accusation; the application addresses the new material specifically</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Non-bailable warrant after absence</td><td>Far harder — the absence must be explained convincingly, and recalling the warrant before the trial court is often the necessary companion step</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Proclamation proceedings initiated</td><td>Settled practice treats proclaimed offenders as disentitled save exceptional cases; timing is everything, so act before Section 84 BNSS machinery starts</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Three practice rules</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Move on knowledge, not on arrest.</strong> The reason to believe standard is forward-looking; waiting until officers are at the door converts a considered application into a midnight scramble.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Engage the chargesheet&rsquo;s contents.</strong> Post-cognizance, the court reads the final report. An application that ignores what the investigator actually alleged forfeits credibility.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Pair protection with participation.</strong> Offer appearance before the trial court on the next date. The order sought is against handcuffs, not against the trial — and courts grant the first far more readily when assured of the second.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer describes general principles for information. It is not legal advice; the viability of a post-chargesheet application depends on the record and the applicant&rsquo;s conduct in each case.</div>
